This paper develops structured irrationality as an extension of the corrective shadow. Human irrationality is usually described through bias, error, heuristic shortcut, motivated reasoning, or insufficient information. Those accounts remain necessary. This paper adds a structural emphasis: reasoning becomes irrational when it serves the preservation of a threatened form more strongly than contact with correction.&nbsp;
<div>
 The personal form is ego-image. The relational form is the shared story. The institutional form is legitimacy. The AI-mediated form is smooth coherence detached from consequence. Each case has its own material and limits. The structural movement repeats across them: a form protects itself, contradiction appears, the contradiction threatens continuity, and the burden of correction is displaced elsewhere.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The corrective shadow names the psychic base of this movement. The ego prefers an image; the persona stabilizes that image socially; projection exports what the image cannot carry; shadow returns as pressure, affect, recurrence, bodily charge, relational conflict, dream, fascination, contempt, or collapse. Structured irrationality appears where reason, morality, diagnosis, procedure, or fluency prevents this return from becoming correction.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper gives special attention to narcissistic organization as a sharp case of burden export. Shame, guilt, dependency, error, limitation, envy, and emptiness are relocated onto others. The defense may sound coherent and socially persuasive while the burden path reveals its irrationality. The problem is therefore moral as well as cognitive: the failure to be wrong changes who pays.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper then develops correction-capacity as the hidden condition of rationality. A person, institution, or AI-mediated workflow can process correction only where some deeper stability allows revision to occur without annihilation, humiliation, collapse, or legitimacy death. Fixed worth is treated as one human form of that stability: the capacity to remain intact when wrong. The institutional analogue is legitimacy that can survive revision. The AI-mediated analogue is coherence bound to verification, consequence, and human answerability.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The conclusion is simple: rationality matures where correction can change the burden path.&nbsp;
</div>

Trust is widely recognized as central to business relationships, yet it has not been formalized as an economic responsiveness variable. While price elasticity and demand elasticity are foundational constructs in economics, no parallel framework has systematically modeled how changes in trust affect conversion behavior, retention dynamics, pricing power, revenue, and profit. As a result, trust remains conceptually important but economically under-specified.&nbsp;
<div>
 This paper introduces Trust Elasticity, a formal managerial economics construct that measures the percentage change in economic outcomes resulting from a one-unit change in trust on a bounded perceptual scale. The framework decomposes trust responsiveness into Conversion, Retention, Pricing, Revenue, and Profit Trust Elasticities and integrates referral-amplified acquisition effects and cost structure mediation within a unified transmission model.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 By modeling trust as a quantifiable economic driver operating across multiple performance channels, the paper establishes a structured foundation for analyzing trust as a strategic variable rather than a purely relational attribute. Worked examples demonstrate how incremental trust improvements can generate economically amplified performance effects under varying revenue compositions and operating structures.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Trust Elasticity provides a formal basis for empirical estimation, cross-industry comparison, and the development of a standardized Trust Elasticity Index. In doing so, it lays the groundwork for a broader economic analysis of trust within competitive markets. This article is intended as a conceptual and methodological contribution for executives and researchers seeking to model trust as an economic variable.
</div>
